# The `evm` CLI

`evm` (package `@camoseed/evm-elf`) answers one question on every chain at once. A
profile is the chain list, and reads fan out across it. `evm --version` says whether
it is installed.

## Reach for it when

- A wallet's balance or nonce is wanted: `evm wallet balance <addr> --json`
- Who owns a contract: `evm contract owner <addr> --json`
- What a proxy is and where it points: `evm contract proxy-info <addr> --json`
  (`-s` scans every chain fast; drop it on the chains that turn out to matter)
- How a contract was deployed, and its salt: `evm contract deploy-info <addr> --json`
- Whether an address has code: `evm contract code <addr> --json`

Write no one-off script for those. There is no generic `call` command, so an
arbitrary view function, event logs and contract source need a different tool.

## Reading the output

- Pass `--json`. It goes to stdout; warnings go to stderr.
- Fan-out commands return an array of per-chain objects. A chain that failed
  carries an `error` field and the exit code is still `0`, so check every entry
  instead of the exit status. Exit `1` means bad arguments, or every chain failed.
- Narrow with `-c base,arbitrum`, or `-xc mainnet`. Chains are queried
  sequentially and the default profile uses public endpoints, so an unnarrowed
  read is a dozen-plus round trips into rate limits.
- `deploy-info` and `proxy-info --full` need an explorer API key. Without one
  those fields are absent and a note lands on stderr — say the key is missing
  rather than reporting the contract as lacking the data.
- Guess no flags: `evm <group> <command> --help` documents each one.

## Before anything broadcasts

`wallet send`, `wallet set-nonce`, `contract transfer-ownership` and
`contract proxy-upgrade` print a plan and stop. `--exec` sends it, with no
confirmation prompt and no undo.

- Never add `--exec` unless the user asked for that transaction, that amount and
  that chain in this conversation. Run the dry run, show it, stop there.
- Pass `-c <chain>` on every write. Without it `wallet send --exec` broadcasts on
  every chain in the profile, and `--all --exec` empties the wallet on each one.
- Pass a key as the name of an env var — `--private-key DEPLOYER_PK` — never as
  hex in the command, which lands in shell history and the process list.
- Repeat none of these outputs back: `wallet generate` prints a mnemonic and a
  private key, and `chain list --json` / `explorer list --json` print stored keys
  unmasked, though their table views mask them.
